<h2>Emergency Roof Tarping: How It Works</h2>
<p>Tarping is the backbone of emergency roof repair. Done right, a tarp keeps a damaged roof dry for weeks while the permanent repair is planned and, if needed, an insurance claim is reviewed. Done badly — a loose tarp held down with a few bricks — it blows off in the next gust and can damage more shingles on the way out.</p>
<p>Our crews use heavy-duty tarps that extend well past the damaged area and over the ridge where possible, secured with wood battens fastened through the tarp so wind cannot get under it. On flat commercial roofs we may use temporary membrane patches or sealants suited to the surface. We photograph the damage before we cover it, so the documentation is not lost under the tarp.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Temporary fix</th><th>When we use it</th><th>How long it typically holds</th></tr></thead>
